Progress
This week we focused on nailing down the overall structure of packetization and clarifying how it should fit into the compiler pipeline.

First, during the Sunday meeting we agreed on a packetization flow:

Use basic blocks.
Basic blocks are sequences of instructions without internal branches.

Use a DAG to track dependencies.
For each basic block, we construct a directed acyclic graph where nodes are instructions and edges represent data or control dependencies. This gives us a clear picture of what can be executed in parallel.

Packetize with several constraints in mind:
– Maintain global load/store order. Loads and stores act as “anchors”: instructions before and after them must preserve their relative order so the program’s memory behavior stays correct.
– Account for functional unit latencies. When we know FU delays, the scheduler/packetizer must respect them so results are ready when needed.
– Minimize total latency. The packetizer should use available parallelism to reduce total cycles while obeying the above constraints.

Progress on the scalar. We can now emit the generated scalar assembly into a .S file without extra logging noise. The main remaining issue is fixing some linking bugs, after which we should be able to produce a working executable.

From the scheduler team’s design review we captured several important constraints and design choices:

– The number of instructions per packet must remain configurable. We should not hardcode “4 instructions per packet” anywhere.
– The number of dependency check bits and set bits (currently 16 and 16) is also not fixed and may change.
– Register allocation must be bank-aware. We have 256 scalar registers split into 4 banks, and the allocator needs to track banks and avoid bank conflicts. This likely requires reading more on multi-bank register allocation techniques.
– Functional unit delays will be specified later and need to be incorporated into scheduling.
– Vector and mask registers are separate physical register types and must be treated distinctly in the backend.

